      "1": "No DOM state shows any Microsoft/MSFT search query, suggestion/result row, or Microsoft quote-page content. The only evidenced search context is for Apple, and the later quote-path page is for AAPL; there is no visible “Microsoft” or “MSFT” anywhere in the provided DOM snippets. Therefore, this criterion can only be scored on whether Microsoft was attempted/located if additional DOM evidence exists beyond what is shown here.",
      "2": "The provided DOM snippets do not show Apple’s company-page metrics sections (no visible ‘Market cap’ label, no visible YTD performance figure, and no Apple-specific news module/headline). One DOM state does show an Apple price and percent change embedded in the search-suggestion row (e.g., “$319.70 +1.63%”), but this appears in an autocomplete/results list rather than a dedicated quote page panel. On the AAPL quote-path state, the visible content still lacks Apple’s price/market cap/YTD and shows only generic news plus Apple-related research prompts; absence is only established for the visible portion of the page, not necessarily the entire page if content requires scrolling/loading.",
      "3": "There is no DOM evidence of a Microsoft company/quote page or Microsoft-specific metrics/news anywhere in the captured states (no share price, market cap, YTD, or Microsoft headline). As a result, Microsoft’s requested items cannot be extracted or verified from these DOM snapshots, and any claim about Microsoft metric visibility/unavailability would need explicit evidence from a Microsoft page state that is not present here.",
      "4": "DOM evidence does not provide both companies’ required fields for comparison: Apple has, at most, a price snippet in search suggestions, while Apple market cap/YTD/headline are not visible in the provided snippets, and Microsoft data is entirely absent. No market-cap value for either company is visible in any provided state, so the ‘which company has the larger market capitalization’ determination cannot be grounded in this DOM evidence. Also, the AAPL quote-path state contains a scrollable region, so non-visibility in the snapshot should be treated as ‘not visible in captured view’ rather than definitive nonexistence."
